---
import Base from '../../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../../../styles/walkthrough.css';

// /metadax/homeschool/walkthrough/ -- "What you'll see" for the parent chair,
// first published 2026-10-10. A numbered sequence: each step is one line of
// what you do and one real screen of what you see. The chat steps (the paste,
// the interview, the next-time note, session two) are rendered as styled
// transcript blocks -- the real text a parent sees. The package, the player
// and the print page are live-page screens; where a PNG was captured it is
// shown, otherwise the figure carries data-shot for the brain to fill.
//
// The worked scenario was run with our own model on a fictional parent: a
// child of eleven who loves football and keeps writing 1/2 + 1/3 = 2/5, pages
// wanted in French. The package it builds is Le Stade des Fractions, the
// finished example linked below. Everyone in it is made up.
//
// Rules on this page: "guide" and "learner"; a way, never the way; no AI
// vendor named (say "your assistant", "the AI subscription you already have");
// no real people, no honorifics; nothing identifying; the Foundation is never
// called a non-profit, charity, or incorporated. Byline and sponsor sentence
// like the other chair pages.
const SHOT = '/metadax/walkthroughs/homeschool';
---
<Base title="What you'll see: a homeschool session, step by step" description="Walk the whole path once: copy the folder, answer about a dozen questions, and get back a page for you and a game for your child. Then the player, the print page, and the note that starts the next one. Free." wide>
  <div class="wt">
  <section class="lede">
    <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/metadax/">Meta DAX</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/metadax/homeschool/">Homeschooling</a> &rsaquo; Walkthrough</p>
    <h1>What you&rsquo;ll see: a homeschool session, from the folder to the next-time note.</h1>
    <p class="lede-text">First published 2026-10-10.</p>
    <p class="body intro">
      This is the whole path, run once on a made-up child so you can see every
      screen before you try it. The child is eleven, lives for football, and
      keeps writing <span class="mono">1/2 + 1/3 = 2/5</span>; the pages are
      wanted in French. What the model built from that is
      <a href="/metadax/samples/stade-des-fractions/">Le Stade des Fractions</a>,
      the finished example you will see below. Everyone in it is made up. This
      is one way through; it is never the only way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <ol class="steps">

      <li class="step" id="s1">
        <p class="do"><span class="n">1</span> Open your AI chat and give it the homeschool skill &mdash; copy the folder in, or paste the one prompt.</p>
        <p class="see">What you see: the assistant reads the folder and says it is ready. No account, no setup.</p>
        <div class="transcript" aria-label="Chat transcript">
          <p class="t-you"><span class="who">You paste</span>I&rsquo;m homeschooling and want to build a session with MetaDAX. Here is the homeschool skill folder &mdash; use it.</p>
          <p class="t-ai"><span class="who">Your assistant</span>Got it &mdash; I&rsquo;ve read the MetaDAX homeschool skill. I&rsquo;ll ask you about a dozen short questions, then build you two things: a page for you to run the session, and a page for your child to use with you beside them. Nothing you tell me is stored anywhere. Please keep out real names, your town, the school, and health details. Ready when you are &mdash; shall we start?</p>
          <p class="t-you"><span class="who">You</span>Yes.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="note">No skill to install? Paste the plain prompt from <a href="https://github.com/daxfoundation/metadax/tree/main/skills/metadax-homeschool">the homeschool skill</a> into any chat and it runs the same interview. Or use the <a href="/metadax/homeschool/start/">guided questionnaire on this site</a> to build your note first and hand that in.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="step" id="s2">
        <p class="do"><span class="n">2</span> Answer the interview. About a dozen short questions, in plain words, one at a time.</p>
        <p class="see">What you see: it asks what your child is stuck on, a real wrong answer, what they love, and how they learn. You answer as a parent, not a form-filler.</p>
        <div class="transcript" aria-label="Chat transcript">
          <p class="t-ai"><span class="who">Your assistant</span>How old is your child, and which subject?</p>
          <p class="t-you"><span class="who">You</span>Eleven. Maths &mdash; fractions.</p>
          <p class="t-ai"><span class="who">Your assistant</span>Where exactly does it keep going wrong? The more exact the better.</p>
          <p class="t-you"><span class="who">You</span>Adding fractions with different bottom numbers. He wrote 1/2 + 1/3 = 2/5.</p>
          <p class="t-ai"><span class="who">Your assistant</span>That one wrong answer tells me a lot &mdash; thank you. What does he love? I build every example and game through it.</p>
          <p class="t-you"><span class="who">You</span>Football. He knows every player and every score.</p>
          <p class="t-ai"><span class="who">Your assistant</span>Anything about how he learns &mdash; as something you see, not a label?</p>
          <p class="t-you"><span class="who">You</span>Hates worksheets. Goes quiet after a wrong answer. Twenty minutes and he&rsquo;s done.</p>
          <p class="t-ai"><span class="who">Your assistant</span>Last few: is there something he could teach you &mdash; a &ldquo;lead&rdquo; moment where he&rsquo;s a step ahead? And which language for the pages?</p>
          <p class="t-you"><span class="who">You</span>He can read a league table better than I can. French, please.</p>
          <p class="t-ai"><span class="who">Your assistant</span>That&rsquo;s everything I need. Building now: a guide page for you and a game page for him, in French, built through football, aimed straight at the 1/2 + 1/3 slip, in short bursts, with one moment where he leads.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="step" id="s3">
        <p class="do"><span class="n">3</span> Read the package it hands back. Two pages: one for you, one for your child.</p>
        <p class="see">What you see: a finished learning pack. The first tab is your guide; the second is your child&rsquo;s game. This is exactly the shape of the finished example &mdash; open it alongside.</p>
        <figure class="shot" data-shot="/metadax/samples/stade-des-fractions/">
          <a class="screen-link" href="/metadax/samples/stade-des-fractions/"><span class="sl-k">Open this screen:</span> Le Stade des Fractions</a>
          <figcaption>The package: <a href="/metadax/samples/stade-des-fractions/">Le Stade des Fractions</a> &mdash; six stations on a football pitch and a running track, a scoreboard in &ldquo;buts&rdquo;, and a <strong>Guide</strong> tab with parent notes for each station (the goal, what to ask, what to watch for, an off-screen activity, a rough time).</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="transcript" aria-label="Chat transcript">
          <p class="t-ai"><span class="who">Your assistant</span>Here is your pack. <strong>Your side</strong> has, for each station: the goal in one line, what to say, what to skip, and the one slip that&rsquo;s normal here (adding the bottoms). <strong>His side</strong> is a game with no AI in it &mdash; he chooses, then sees; every wrong answer is matched to the exact mistake behind it. Station&nbsp;2 is built so the rink&hellip; sorry, the pitch is cut two ways and lined up, which is the picture that kills 1/2 + 1/3 = 2/5. Station&nbsp;5 is his lead: he reads the table, you follow.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="step" id="s4">
        <p class="do"><span class="n">4</span> Open your child&rsquo;s side in the player.</p>
        <p class="see">What you see: the game page running on its own, full screen, no chat anywhere near it. He chooses first, then sees whether it was right, and why.</p>
        <figure class="shot" data-shot="/metadax/play/">
          <a class="screen-link" href="/metadax/play/"><span class="sl-k">Open this screen:</span> Play a MetaDAX package</a>
          <figcaption>The player at <a href="/metadax/play/">/metadax/play/</a>: the learner&rsquo;s page on its own &mdash; a 3D scene, the question, the choices, and feedback tied to the exact mistake. It gets harder after a streak and easier after a miss, and keeps nothing but a game badge in your own browser.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </li>

      <li class="step" id="s5">
        <p class="do"><span class="n">5</span> Print it for a screen-free session.</p>
        <p class="see">What you see: the same pack laid out for paper &mdash; the stations, the questions, and your guide notes, ready for the printer.</p>
        <figure class="shot" data-shot="/metadax/play/print.html">
          <a class="screen-link" href="/metadax/play/print.html"><span class="sl-k">Open this screen:</span> Print a MetaDAX package</a>
          <figcaption>The print page at <a href="/metadax/play/print.html">/metadax/play/print.html</a>: the whole pack on paper, so the twenty minutes can happen at the kitchen table with a pencil instead of a screen.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </li>

      <li class="step" id="s6">
        <p class="do"><span class="n">6</span> After the session, write the next-time note. Three or four lines, a nickname only.</p>
        <p class="see">What you see: a short note you fill in and keep. Nothing leaves your device unless you copy it.</p>
        <div class="transcript mono-block" aria-label="The next-time note">
<pre>MetaDAX next-time note
Ref: HS-7KQ2
Session: 1

What the learner did:   Added fractions with the same bottom number; tried a
                        few with different bottoms.
What worked:            Cutting the pitch into equal zones &mdash; &ldquo;halves have to
                        be the same size&rdquo; clicked.
Misconceptions seen:    Added tops and bottoms (1/2 + 1/3 = 2/5) x3.
The trail (what I saw): Lit up at the pitch; went quiet after the first miss,
                        came back when I let him read the table to me.
Interests:              Football &mdash; still landing, keep it.
Only I could:           Noticed the quiet and backed off; let him lead.
Stopped:                Finished, but the last two were a stretch &mdash; keep it
                        short next time.</pre>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="step" id="s7">
        <p class="do"><span class="n">7</span> Next time, paste the note at the start. Session two comes back shaped by it.</p>
        <p class="see">What you see: the assistant opens by recalling what worked, moves exactly one step onto the slip, keeps football, and keeps it short &mdash; because your note told it to.</p>
        <div class="transcript" aria-label="Chat transcript">
          <p class="t-you"><span class="who">You paste</span>Here is my MetaDAX next-time note from last session, build the next one. <span class="dim">(the note above)</span></p>
          <p class="t-ai"><span class="who">Your assistant</span>Picking up from session one. We&rsquo;ll open by remembering that equal-sized zones clicked, then take one step: why you can&rsquo;t just add the bottoms &mdash; cut the pitch two ways and line them up, aimed straight at the 1/2 + 1/3 = 2/5 slip. Still football. Shorter and gentler, since the end was a stretch last time. Here is session two&hellip;</p>
        </div>
      </li>

    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="h-time">
    <h2 id="h-time">How long this took</h2>
    <p class="body">About twenty-five minutes end to end on our run: five to hand over the skill and answer the interview, a couple while it built, and the rest reading the pack and trying his side before the session. The session itself is the twenty minutes you were always going to spend &mdash; the preparing is what it took off you.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="h-wrong">
    <h2 id="h-wrong">When it goes wrong</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>It asks for a name, or you nearly type one.</strong> It shouldn&rsquo;t, and it won&rsquo;t keep one. Use a nickname or &ldquo;my son&rdquo;. On our run it caught a town name we&rsquo;d typed and asked us to drop it.</li>
      <li><strong>The pack feels generic.</strong> That means the stuck spot was too broad. Go back and give the one real wrong answer (&ldquo;1/2 + 1/3 = 2/5&rdquo;), not &ldquo;fractions&rdquo;. It is the single most useful line you can give it.</li>
      <li><strong>The game won&rsquo;t open in the player.</strong> Save the file your assistant gave you with a <span class="mono">.json</span> name and drop it into <a href="/metadax/play/">the player</a>; if it still won&rsquo;t, print it instead &mdash; the paper version needs nothing.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="h-run">
    <h2 id="h-run">Run it yourself, free</h2>
    <p class="body">
      It runs on the AI subscription you already have. Use the
      <a href="/metadax/homeschool/start/">guided questionnaire</a> to build your
      note, or drop <a href="https://github.com/daxfoundation/metadax/tree/main/skills/metadax-homeschool">the
      homeschool skill</a> into your assistant and it knows the whole flow.
    </p>
    <h2 id="h-done" class="done-head">Have it done for you</h2>
    <p class="body">
      Want it set up and run for you instead? <a href="https://www.obsidiandelta.com/learning/">Obsidian
      Delta, the company, sells that</a> &mdash; done-for-you, paid. The
      Foundation&rsquo;s work stays free; this is the way to have someone else do
      the work.
    </p>
    <p class="body sponsor">
      MetaDAX is free. It is built on paid compute. If it helped, a coffee on
      <a href="https://ko-fi.com/jeyanandan">Ko-fi</a> or a
      <a href="https://github.com/sponsors/ObsidianDelta">GitHub sponsorship</a>
      keeps it going.
    </p>
    <p class="out">
      <a href="/metadax/homeschool/">Back to Homeschooling</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
      <a href="/metadax/samples/stade-des-fractions/">See the finished example</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
      <a href="/metadax/">Pick your chair</a>
    </p>
  </section>
  </div>
</Base>
